  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124574,
      "id": "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855",
      "title": "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cb856f47-ca19-5b11-878e-39b103011d1c",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 4 x y^{3} z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z - 6 z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 2 x^{2} y^{3} - 12 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 4 x y^{3} z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z - 6 z^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} - 12 y z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 4 x y^{3} z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z - 6 z^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 6 x^{2} y^{2} z - 6 z^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 6 z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 6 z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- 6 y z^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- 6 y z^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} z - 6 y z^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} - 12 y z+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=0 \\\\ h(z)&=C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} y^{3} z - 6 y z^{2}+C.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "72c360e7-7863-5acc-800c-afbe76855758",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
